  "result": "I have always liked the quiet authority of public benches. They ask nothing, promise little, and somehow become stages for the smallest human dramas. A bench beside a river may hold a tired runner at dawn, a pair of friends at noon, and someone nursing a private disappointment after sunset.\n\nUnlike chairs indoors, benches belong partly to weather. Rain darkens their boards. Pollen gathers in their corners. Winter makes them seem inhospitable, while summer turns them into islands of shade. Even an empty bench contains evidence: a forgotten receipt, a scratched initial, a crumb being inspected by an ant.\n\nPerhaps that is why benches encourage observation. Sitting still in public gives the world permission to continue without you. Bicycles pass. Dogs negotiate invisible borders. Children invent rules, revise them, and accuse one another of cheating. Strangers offer fragments of conversation to their phones, leaving you to imagine the missing lives.\n\nA bench also represents a modest kind of civic faith. Someone decided that people would need rest here. Someone measured the space, planted trees, tightened bolts, and expected the place to be shared. The gesture is ordinary, yet generous.\n\nIf I could dedicate a bench, I would avoid a grand inscription. I might choose: Sit awhile; nothing important requires your immediate attention. That sentence would not always be true, but for ten minutes it might feel true enough. And sometimes a brief illusion of peace is not an escape from life, but a simple way of returning to it refreshed.",
